Windows: skip FEX's own memory notifications raised during a code invalidation

A code invalidation runs FEXCore code that frees and commits FEX's own
memory: InvalidateThreadCachedCodeRange resets the call-ret stack
(a decommit of up to 16 MB), the lookup caches drop pages, and a code
buffer whose last reference goes away is released. On the iOS host Wine
reports those calls back to FEX as memory notifications on the same
thread. InvalidateAlignedInterval then invalidates again while the outer
invalidation still holds CodeBufferListLock (a std::mutex, not
recursive): the thread parks on that lock forever while write-owning
CodeInvalidationMutex, and every other thread queues behind it on the
code lock. With MADEIRA_INVALIDATION_NESTED=0 the same thread parks on
CodeInvalidationMutex itself instead.

Seen on .NET games since 0.2.0 (Stardew Valley 4 of 4 runs, Slay the
Spire 2 in most logs): one [inval-nested] "invalidate" line from
InvalidateAlignedInterval, then the owner parked on CTX+0x530
(CodeBufferListLock) with [fexlock] STUCK read-waits behind it.

InvalidateIntervalInternalLocked now records the thread that is inside
it, and HandleMemoryProtectionNotification, InvalidateAlignedInterval
and InvalidateContainingSection return at once for notifications raised
on that thread while it is there, like the IntervalsLock re-entry skip.
Only FEX's own code runs inside an invalidation, so such a notification
is never about guest code. The skips are counted and reported from
HandleImageMap as [inval-own-mem].

Applies on FEX a77d5687 after 0001. Madeira-only: not for
FEX-Emu.

